From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 04:13:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 159/342] scheduler: give up on interrupts that can't move to
 another CPU

Power saving mode packs interrupts on one core: pack_irqs() moved the
interrupts of the current CPU one by one until its list was empty. When
the architecture can't route an interrupt elsewhere (arm64's
arch_int_assign_to_cpu() keeps every one on CPU 0), the interrupt went
back onto the same list, so the loop never ended, in the scheduler, with
interrupts disabled: choosing "Power saving" in ProcessController hung
an ODROID-C2 (CPU 0 stuck, KDL unreachable). It now stops when an
interrupt stays. CPUEntry::Stop(), which moves the interrupts away from
a CPU being disabled, had the same loop.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/system/kernel/scheduler/power_saving.cpp  | 6 ++++++
 src/system/kernel/scheduler/scheduler_cpu.cpp | 5 +++++
 2 files changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/system/kernel/scheduler/power_saving.cpp b/src/system/kernel/scheduler/power_saving.cpp
index 600c1cd471..c138e0b2e0 100644
--- a/src/system/kernel/scheduler/power_saving.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/scheduler/power_saving.cpp
@@ -215,6 +215,12 @@ pack_irqs()
 
 		if (newCPU != cpu->cpu_num)
 			assign_io_interrupt_to_cpu(irq->irq, newCPU);
+		if (irq->cpu == cpu->cpu_num) {
+			// It stayed: the architecture can't route it elsewhere (arm64
+			// keeps every interrupt on CPU 0). Trying the next ones would
+			// go on forever, with interrupts disabled.
+			return;
+		}
 
 		locker.Lock();
 	}
diff --git a/src/system/kernel/scheduler/scheduler_cpu.cpp b/src/system/kernel/scheduler/scheduler_cpu.cpp
index 380b7ffcf3..d1b00442bc 100644
--- a/src/system/kernel/scheduler/scheduler_cpu.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/scheduler/scheduler_cpu.cpp
@@ -117,6 +117,11 @@ CPUEntry::Stop()
 		locker.Unlock();
 
 		assign_io_interrupt_to_cpu(irq->irq, -1);
+		if (irq->cpu == fCPUNumber) {
+			// It stayed: the architecture can't route it elsewhere (arm64
+			// keeps every interrupt on CPU 0), and the others would stay too.
+			return;
+		}
 
 		locker.Lock();
 		irq = entry->irqs.First();
